Subject: [PATCH] feat(reference): publish the worked example and the working
 files behind it
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0012. Two things lose deals that the physics never touches: a prospect
cannot see what €20k buys until after they have paid for it, and a client who
has paid cannot check the arithmetic without taking our word for it. Both are
now closed, by the same mechanism.

THE WORKED EXAMPLE (/reference)

A complete Envelope Study for a synthetic hall, published in full: the document,
the walkthrough deck, the proposal, the model pack, and the CSV bundle behind
them. Same code path as a paid deliverable, nothing removed, nothing dressed up.
The page leads with the three numbers a colo operator actually argues about —
racks deployable today, after the grid-only ladder, and with behind-the-meter —
and ends at /qualify.

Generated by scripts/build-reference.sh from examples/intake/reference_hall.json.
The intake is invented and says so: model_pack.json carries "synthetic": true and
a notice in words, because the pack is downloadable and will be separated from
the page it came from. A modelled figure that travels without its label
eventually gets quoted as a measurement. That is the one failure this business
does not survive, so CI asserts the notice is present.

THE WORKING FILES

csv_bundle() emits six tables and a README: every scenario, every rung of the
headroom ladder with its cost and lead time, all thirteen constraints (not only
the binding one, with 'binds' stated rather than left to be inferred from the
minimum), every input with 'client' or 'assumed' against it, the gap ledger, and
a provenance row per derived quantity with its digest and the digests it came
from. Anyone can rebuild the study from those files. That is the intent — the
moat is not opacity, it is that reproducing them requires the engine.

Delivered studies carry the same bundle (migration 0006, working_files jsonb),
served per-file from /deliverable/<token>?format=csv&file=<name>. Admin list
views strip it to a has_working_files flag so it is never fetched by accident.

A DETERMINISTIC CLOCK, SO CI CAN TELL DRIFT FROM RE-DATING

gridforge/clock.py pins the report date through GRIDFORGE_REPORT_DATE, and the
reference zip is stamped with a fixed timestamp. Two runs of the same commit are
now byte-identical, which is what makes the new `reference` CI job meaningful: it
regenerates public/reference and fails on git diff --exit-code. A published
worked example that has quietly drifted from the engine is worse than none — it
is a specific, checkable promise we are no longer keeping, in public, on a page
we sent prospects to.

Also: the markdown deliverable now carries its generation date (it did not; the
HTML did), and ends with a newline.

TESTS

tests/test_working_files.py — 16 tests. The provenance chain must close (no
derived_from digest may reference a quantity absent from the bundle), every
scenario must reach the table with an AACE class, exactly one constraint per
scenario may be marked binding, every gap must carry how_to_get_it, the blank
intake must still produce a bundle, two pinned runs must be byte-identical, and
the shipped artefacts must exist, declare themselves synthetic, and contain no
measurement language.

Suite: 171 passed. tsc clean, next lint clean, next build clean with /reference
in the route table.

